d pre-1900 source found, so the stored value stays for now. Moisture: stored value clear (a definite Drying), so it stays Dry.", "rule_general": "FINAL founder rules 2026-09-15: clear stored modern value stays; unclear or missing value set by majority of first-hand pre-1900 sources; tie broken by the oldest first-hand written source, Neutral if the oldest sources are themselves tied; copied readings count once; group statements, second-hand reports, other parts and other species shown but not counted; snigdha and 潤 = Moist, rūkṣa and 燥 = Dry.", "sources": [{"agreement": "Shown for context. Two reasons not to count it. The tree is Juglans regia, the European walnut, not the American Juglans nigra; and the reading is for the kernel, while the stored parts are the hull, the leaf and the bark.", "assigns_moisture": "Moist", "assigns_temperature": "Cold", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "A. The fresh kernels of the nuts newly gathered are pleasant to the taste: they are a little cold, and have no small moisture, which is not perfectly concocted. B. The dry nuts are hot and dry, and those more which become oily and rank.", "gloss": "Fresh walnut kernels are a little cold and very moist; dried walnuts are hot and dry.", "locator": "Book III, Chap. 90 'Of the Walnut Tree', The Temperature and Virtues", "not_counted_why": "Two reasons not to count it. The tree is Juglans regia, the European walnut, not the American Juglans nigra; and the reading is for the kernel, while the stored parts are the hull, the leaf and the bark.", "note": "Two reasons not to count it. The tree is Juglans regia, the European walnut, not the American Juglans nigra; and the reading is for the kernel, while the stored parts are the hull, the leaf and the bark.", "short": "Gerard (1633)", "source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged by Thomas Johnson (1633 text; read in the Ex-Classics t