group statements, second-hand reports, other parts and other species shown but not counted; snigdha and 潤 = Moist, rūkṣa and 燥 = Dry.", "sources": [{"agreement": "Shown for context. His two vervains are the common upright and the creeping European kinds, both Verbena officinalis and its relatives. A different species from the stored Verbena hastata, so it is shown and not counted.", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Cool", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "Both the Vervains are of temperature very dry, and do meanly bind and cool.", "gloss": "Both vervains are very dry and moderately binding and cooling.", "locator": "Book II, Chap. 247 'Of Vervain', The Temperature", "not_counted_why": "His two vervains are the common upright and the creeping European kinds, both Verbena officinalis and its relatives. A different species from the stored Verbena hastata, so it is shown and not counted.", "note": "His two vervains are the common upright and the creeping European kinds, both Verbena officinalis and its relatives. A different species from the stored Verbena hastata, so it is shown and not counted.", "short": "Gerard (1633)", "source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged by Thomas Johnson (1633 text; read in the Ex-Classics transcription and checked against the 1636 printing scanned at archive.org)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt", "year": "1633"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. The European Verbena officinalis, a different species from the stored plant.", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "Verbena. Vervain: hot and dry, a great opener, cleanser, healer, it helps the yellow jaundice, defects in the reins and bladder, pains in the head", "gloss": "Vervain is hot and dry.", "locator": "Catalogue of simples, Herbs, 'Verbena' (Gutenberg #49513, offset ~899270); Culpeper's degree tables, 'Herbs' (Gutenberg #49513, offset ~945500), where Vervain is listed hot in the second degree and dry in the second degree", "not_counted_why": "The European Verbena officinalis, a different species from the stored plant.", "note": "The European Verbena officinalis, a different species from the stored plant.", "short": "Culpeper (1653)", "source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (text of 1653; London: Thomas Kelly, 1850 edition as digitised by Project Gutenberg #49513)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt", "year": "1653"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. Salmon's Kinds section lists three, and the third is 'Verbena Peruana, Verbena Americana, Verbena Caroliniana ... American, or Peruvian Vervain', which he says he found three times in South Carolina. His one sentence covers all three kinds together, and he never says which American plant his third kind is, so it cannot be tied to Verbena hastata. Shown and not counted.", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "VIW. The Qualities. Vervain is hot in the firft degree, and dry in the fecond: Aperitive, Abfter- five, Anodyn, Diuretick and Vulnerary", "gloss": "Vervain is hot in the first degree and dry in the second.", "locator": "Chap. DCCXXI 'Of VERVAIN', The Qualities (OCR at offset 6343800; the chapter opens at offset 6341345)", "not_counted_why": "Salmon's Kinds section lists three, and the third is 'Verbena Peruana, Verbena Americana, Verbena Caroliniana ... American, or Peruvian Vervain', which he says he found three times in South Carolina. His one sentence covers all three kinds together, and he never says which American plant his third kind is, so it cannot be tied to Verbena hastata. Shown and not counted.", "note": "Salmon's Kinds section lists three, and the third is 'Verbena Peruana, Verbena Americana, Verbena Caroliniana ... American, or Peruvian Vervain', which he says he found three times in South Carolina. His one sentence covers all three kinds together, and he never says which American plant his third kind is, so it cannot be tied to Verbena hastata. Shown and not counted.", "short": "Salmon (1710)", "source": "William Salmon, Botanologia: The English Herbal (London, 1710)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://archive.org/download/gri_33125012917387/gri_33125012917387_djvu.txt", "year": "1710"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. The Chinese ma bian cao is Verbena officinalis, a different species from the stored Verbena hastata. Shown and not counted. Its cold reading happens to agree with the stored Cool, but it does not stand behind it.", "assigns_moisture": null, "assigns_temperature": "Cold", "corpus": "chinese", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "馬鞭 瀉、破血、消胀、殺虫。味苦微寒。破血通經。殺虫消胀。", "gloss": "Ma bian cao: drains, breaks blood stasis, reduces distension, kills worms. Taste bitter, nature SLIGHTLY COLD.", "locator": "卷三 草部, 馬鞭 (congxin.txt offset 39794)", "not_counted_why": "The Chinese ma bian cao is Verbena officinalis, a different species from the stored Verbena hastata. Shown and not counted. Its cold reading happens to agree with the stored Cool, but it does not stand behind it.", "note": "The Chinese ma bian cao is Verbena officinalis, a different species from the stored Verbena hastata. Shown and not counted. Its cold reading happens to agree with the stored Cool, but it does not stand behind it.", "short": "Bencao Congxin (1757)", "source": "吳儀洛, 本草從新 (Wu Yiluo, Bencao Congxin)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Chinese", "url": "https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/本草從新", "year": "1757"}], "sources_agree": false, "stored_before": {"moisture": "Drying", "temperature": "Cool"}, "stored_value_status": {"moisture": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Drying"}, "temperature": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Cool"}}}$j$::jsonb);

